WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.659
A reference is only meaningful because
some value exists for it to refer to.

2
00:00:03.739 --> 00:00:04.742
That value has an owner.

3
00:00:04.822 --> 00:00:07.731
The owner is responsible for cleanup when
the value goes out of scope.

4
00:00:07.811 --> 00:00:10.319
A borrower may read or modify the value
through a reference,

5
00:00:10.399 --> 00:00:13.157
but the borrower does not control the
lifetime of the owned value.

6
00:00:13.237 --> 00:00:15.794
Shared references allow read-only access
to borrowed data.

7
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:18.131
Mutable references allow controlled
modification.

8
00:00:18.211 --> 00:00:21.571
In both cases, the reference depends on
the owner keeping the value alive for the

9
00:00:21.651 --> 00:00:22.654
duration of the borrow.

10
00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:25.091
Lifetimes describe that relationship at
compile time.

11
00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:27.879
A lifetime is not a countdown timer and it
is not a runtime object.

12
00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:29.815
It is a way for Rust to reason about
validity.

13
00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:31.550
The compiler is not measuring seconds.

14
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.990
It is checking whether the owner of the
data is guaranteed to live long enough for

15
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.223
the reference that uses it.

16
00:00:36.303 --> 00:00:38.359
This distinction helps developers design
APIs.

17
00:00:38.439 --> 00:00:41.599
A function that borrows from an input
should make that relationship clear.

18
00:00:41.679 --> 00:00:44.888
A function that creates new data and
returns it may need to return owned data.

19
00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:47.626
A struct that stores references should
show, through its type,

20
00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:49.662
that it cannot outlive the data it
references.

21
00:00:49.742 --> 00:00:53.152
Security and reliability improve when
owner and borrower responsibilities are

22
00:00:53.232 --> 00:00:53.633
visible.

23
00:00:53.713 --> 00:00:57.274
Hidden assumptions about who owns data and
how long references remain valid are hard

24
00:00:57.354 --> 00:00:57.805
to review.

25
00:00:57.885 --> 00:00:59.390
Clear lifetimes, clear ownership,

26
00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.980
and clear function signatures turn those
assumptions into engineering evidence.
